From c71b935083dc6d09652df6ed08bcae814dbab6f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Petar Kirov <petar.p.kirov@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 11:16:55 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] [SPIRV] Buffer device addresses on request; index them with
 OpPtrAccessChain

On top of llvm/llvm-project#216919 (PhysicalStorageBuffer addressing for
Vulkan), which mapped address space 1 to PhysicalStorageBuffer for every
target that is not a kernel. That turned the global pointers of plain
`spirv64-unknown-unknown` modules into buffer device addresses as well, and,
with an unconditional PhysicalStorageBufferAddresses and Int8 requirement on
every OpConvertUToPtr and OpConvertPtrToU, broke 35 CodeGen/SPIRV tests; six
more pin address space 1 of a shader to CrossWorkgroup.

- A shader module asks for buffer device addresses with the module flag
  `spirv.physical-storage-buffer` (as `spirv.signed-integers` asks for signed
  integers). Only then is address space 1 PhysicalStorageBuffer, the
  addressing model PhysicalStorageBuffer64 and SPV_KHR_physical_storage_buffer
  available; every other module is as upstream has it, and every upstream
  test passes again.
- The capability rules are upstream's again: OpInBoundsPtrAccessChain and
  OpCopyMemorySized need Addresses, OpConvertUToPtr and OpConvertPtrToU need
  nothing of their own, and Int8 is never added (Vulkan grants it only with
  a feature). An OpTypeForwardPointer to PhysicalStorageBuffer needs that
  storage class's capability instead of Addresses.
- A GEP that offsets a PhysicalStorageBuffer pointer, `p[i]`, was emitted as
  an OpAccessChain with the offset as its first index, which spirv-val
  rejects ("reached non-composite type"). It is an OpPtrAccessChain, never the
  in-bounds variant (which needs Addresses), and the pointer type carries the
  ArrayStride Vulkan requires of its base. A GEP whose first index is a
  constant zero stays an OpAccessChain without it.
- The PR's own test named its resource with a null pointer, which this
  revision asserts on; it names it.

